# build artifacts / binaries
/dist/
/awtest
/worker-bin/
.last-image

# Release staging dirs (rel4, rel5, rel10, ...). These are scratch output from
# manual release runs; they accumulated untracked and would otherwise be swept
# into the next `git add -A`.
/rel[0-9]*/

# agent-toolchain: multi-GB pre-downloaded build artifacts (rebuilt by
# agent-toolchain/build-toolchain.sh -> fetch-artifacts.sh); never committed.
/agent-toolchain/cache/

# agent-toolchain: publish-artifacts.sh staging (built relocatable tarballs).
/agent-toolchain/.publish/

# go
go.work.sum
*.db
*.test

# os / editor
.DS_Store
*.swp
.idea/
.vscode/

# agent-toolchain VM/Android staged guest disks (multi-GB).
# NOTE: only the staged disk images are ignored. The small UEFI boot-support
# files ARE committed (main tracks macos/xcode/support/* and
# windows/devtools/disk-support/*), so do NOT ignore the whole xcode/ or
# devtools/ directories — that would drop them from every snapshot.
/agent-toolchain/vm/macos/base/
/agent-toolchain/vm/windows/base/
/agent-toolchain/vm/macos/xcode/disk/
/agent-toolchain/vm/windows/devtools/disk/
/agent-toolchain/vm/*/repack-*/
# The compiled binary at the repo root — ANCHORED.
#
# The bare pattern `easyworker` matched at ANY depth, so it also ignored
# cmd/easyworker/ — which is the worker's main package. The directory was
# therefore never committed: `git status` was clean, the source was on disk, and
# every image build and CI run failed at
#
#   go build ./cmd/easyworker   -> no such directory
#
# while nothing anywhere said a file was missing. A leading slash constrains the
# pattern to the repo root, which is what was intended.
/easyworker
dist/
